// Dedicated original checkpoint bank; never imports lesson or practice questions.
// Row shape: Messer lesson ID, scenario, four options, correct index, explanation.
const MC_OBJECTIVES=[
 ['1.1','Security controls'],['1.2','Security concepts'],['1.3','Change management'],['1.4','Cryptographic solutions'],
 ['2.1','Threat actors'],['2.2','Threat vectors and attack surfaces'],['2.3','Vulnerabilities']
].map(([id,title])=>({id,title,domain:Number(id.split('.')[0])}));
const MC_BANK=[];
function mcBankAdd(objective,rows){rows.forEach((r,i)=>MC_BANK.push({id:`mc-${objective}-${String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}`,objective,lesson:r[0],prompt:r[1],options:r[2],answer:r[3],why:r[4]}));}
mcBankAdd('1.1',[
 ['security-controls','A retailer can fund either a report of unusual refunds or a rule that blocks refunds over an employee’s limit. Its immediate requirement is to stop unauthorized refunds before processing. Which choice best meets it?',['The report, as a corrective control','The blocking rule, as a preventive control','The report, as a compensating control','The blocking rule, as a detective control'],1,'Blocking the transaction enforces the limit before loss. A report may reveal violations afterward but does not meet the prevention requirement by itself.'],
 ['security-controls','An overnight guard walks through a warehouse and reports doors left open. Classify the patrol itself by category and primary purpose.',['Managerial and directive','Technical and corrective','Physical and compensating','Operational and detective'],3,'The patrol is a human-run process that discovers a condition. The door hardware is physical; the policy requiring patrols is managerial.'],
 ['security-controls','An old production machine cannot run the mandated endpoint agent. An approved isolated gateway restricts and monitors its traffic until replacement. Why is the gateway described as compensating?',['It reduces the risk through an alternative to the required safeguard','It eliminates the need to assess the machine','It merely records acceptance of the risk','It repairs an already infected machine'],0,'Compensating controls address a requirement through an acceptable alternative. Approval and risk coverage matter; an exception without a safeguard would be risk acceptance.'],
 ['security-controls','A security review recommends an employee conduct policy, supervisor checks and automated account restrictions. Which component is primarily managerial?',['The automated restrictions','The software producing alerts','The conduct policy','The supervisor performing daily checks'],2,'The policy establishes governance and expectations. People executing checks are operational; software enforcing restrictions is technical.'],
 ['security-controls','An intruder has already altered a workstation. Which action most clearly serves a corrective function?',['Posting a warning at the entrance','Reimaging the workstation from a verified clean source','Recording its serial number','Requiring badges for future visitors'],1,'Reimaging restores the damaged system to a known state. Warnings deter and badges prevent entry; inventory alone does not correct the compromise.'],
 ['security-controls','A company posts “All removable storage must be approved by IT.” No mechanism enforces it. What function does this instruction primarily serve?',['Detective','Corrective','Directive','Compensating'],2,'It directs required behavior. Issuing a rule is different from detecting or technically preventing violations of that rule.'],
 ['security-controls','A loading dock has a steel barrier and a sign threatening prosecution. Which comparison is accurate?',['Both are necessarily corrective','The sign physically stops vehicles','The barrier is managerial and the sign is technical','The barrier can prevent entry; the sign is intended to deter it'],3,'A barrier can obstruct access, while a warning seeks to change behavior through perceived consequences. Neither is a restoration action.'],
 ['security-controls','A mail system delivers suspicious messages and only sends the security team an alert. Management calls this preventive filtering. What is the flaw in that description?',['Alerting without stopping delivery primarily detects the event','Email controls cannot be technical','Every alert is automatically corrective','Preventive controls must be physical devices'],0,'Evaluate what the control does in the stated configuration. Detection can support later response but is not the same as blocking delivery.'],
 ['security-controls','A locked cabinet protects backup media. A technician signs media in and out using a checklist. Which pairing describes these control categories?',['Cabinet: managerial; checklist process: technical','Cabinet: physical; checklist process: operational','Cabinet: detective; checklist process: deterrent','Cabinet: operational; checklist process: physical'],1,'The cabinet is a physical safeguard. The technician’s procedure is operational. Category and purpose are separate ways of classifying controls.'],
 ['security-controls','The board requires an annual risk assessment before approving major projects. What is the best category for this governance requirement?',['Technical','Physical','Managerial','Corrective'],2,'Governance requirements and risk decisions are managerial controls. An assessment tool used to carry them out would be a separate technical component.'],
 ['security-controls','A team claims a backup is enough because it can recover encrypted files after ransomware. Which objective does that backup most directly support when used for restoration?',['Deterring the attacker before contact','Directing employee behavior','Preventing every initial infection','Correcting the impact of damaged data'],3,'Restoring a clean backup helps correct an incident’s effects. Backups do not replace controls that prevent or detect initial compromise.'],
 ['security-controls','A software rule rejects unsigned scripts. A written standard says only approved scripts may run. How do their implementation categories differ?',['Rule: technical; standard: managerial','Rule: operational; standard: physical','Rule: physical; standard: technical','Both are operational because employees use them'],0,'Software enforces the technical rule; the standard sets the managerial requirement. A control’s users do not determine its implementation category.'],
 ['security-controls','A receptionist refuses entry when a visitor cannot identify a sponsor. In this situation, what is the control’s primary function?',['Detective, because the visitor was observed','Preventive, because entry was denied','Corrective, because the visit ended','Compensating, because a person made the decision'],1,'The stated action stops unauthorized access. A human-run control can be preventive; it is not automatically compensating just because it uses a person.'],
 ['security-controls','A firm cannot immediately replace a weak lock. It approves staffed access verification offering equivalent protection for the interim. What distinguishes this from simply documenting an exception?',['A staff member can never be a control','The original requirement disappears permanently','Another safeguard actively addresses the underlying access risk','The exception itself prevents entry'],2,'A compensating safeguard provides risk reduction. Documenting inability to meet a requirement is not protection on its own.'],
 ['security-controls','A camera’s visible placement discourages theft, and investigators later use its footage. Which conclusion best describes this control?',['A control must have exactly one function in all situations','Recording makes it purely managerial','Visibility makes it purely corrective','Its deterrent and detective functions depend on the use being examined'],3,'One implementation can serve multiple purposes. Classify the function emphasized by the scenario rather than memorizing one label for every camera.'],
 ['security-controls','An audit identifies that staff know a rule but no one checks compliance. Which addition most directly supplies a detective control?',['Periodic documented sampling of actual compliance','A revised statement of the same rule','A poster restating penalties','A replacement incident recovery image'],0,'Sampling actual behavior can reveal violations. Repeating expectations is directive or deterrent; a recovery image serves a different function.']
]);
mcBankAdd('1.2',[
 ['cia-triad','A clinic’s appointment portal stays online, but an attacker silently changes appointment times. Which goal is directly violated even though availability remains intact?',['Confidentiality','Integrity','Non-repudiation','Redundancy'],1,'The records no longer accurately reflect the authorized schedule. Integrity concerns unauthorized alteration; staying online does not guarantee correct data.'],
 ['cia-triad','A shipping system rejects every legitimate request during a traffic flood. An investigation finds no data disclosure or modification. Which goal needs immediate restoration?',['Confidentiality','Authentication','Availability','Non-repudiation'],2,'Legitimate users cannot use the service. The absence of theft or modification does not prevent an availability incident.'],
 ['cia-triad','A contractor can read sensitive designs through an accidentally broad permission. No file has been changed. Which security goal is most directly at risk?',['Integrity only','Availability only','Accounting only','Confidentiality'],3,'Excessive read access risks unauthorized disclosure. Integrity and availability are separate concerns, even when the same permissions system supports them.'],
 ['non-repudiation','A project manager disputes having approved a design. The company kept only a screenshot of a typed name. Which replacement workflow provides stronger cryptographic evidence?',['An individually controlled signing key linked to a verified identity','A shared team password below the typed name','A shorter document with the same name','Encryption only with the archive server’s public key'],0,'An attributable digital signature binds the approval content to the signing key. Shared credentials and a typed name provide weaker evidence of who approved it.'],
 ['non-repudiation','An auditor receives a signed expense claim but only has the recipient’s public key. What additional key is needed to verify the claimed signer’s signature?',['The recipient’s private key','The signer’s trusted public verification key','The signer’s private key','A secret key shared by all auditors'],1,'Verification uses the signer’s public key. Auditors should not need access to the private signing key, which must remain protected.'],
 ['non-repudiation','A manager’s private signing key was copied by an attacker before a disputed approval. What is the best interpretation of a valid signature on that approval?',['It proves the manager personally approved the document','It proves the document was confidential','It proves use of that key, but compromise weakens attribution to the manager','It means the certificate can never be revoked'],2,'Signature verification alone cannot distinguish the legitimate key holder from someone who stole the key. Key custody is part of meaningful attribution.'],
 ['aaa','A reporting user can log in, view dashboards and run queries, but cannot create new administrator accounts. Which AAA function enforces that last restriction?',['Authentication','Accounting','Identification','Authorization'],3,'Authorization decides what an authenticated identity may do. Accounting records actions, and authentication establishes the identity claim.'],
 ['aaa','A support engineer needs to establish when an account connected and how many records it exported. Which evidence best answers those questions?',['Time-stamped session and activity logs tied to the account','The user’s job title alone','The password-complexity policy','A list of trusted certificate roots'],0,'Accounting records resource use and activity. Policies and titles describe expectations or permissions, not what happened during a session.'],
 ['aaa','A user successfully authenticates to an identity provider but has no assigned role in a new application. What should the application do?',['Grant administrative access because the identity provider is trusted','Apply its access policy and deny ungranted permissions','Disable logging for that account','Request the identity provider’s private key'],1,'Authentication can be delegated while authorization remains the application’s responsibility. A known identity is not permission to perform every action.'],
 ['gap-analysis','A company documents that only half its servers meet the approved hardening baseline. What did comparing inventory evidence with the baseline produce?',['Proof that all noncompliant servers were exploited','A completed mitigation','A gap between current and required security state','A replacement for risk prioritization'],2,'Gap analysis identifies the difference. Exploitation, risk ranking and remediation require additional evidence or action.'],
 ['gap-analysis','Two gaps remain: an exposed admin service and a missing cosmetic banner. What is the strongest basis for deciding the remediation order?',['Which task was entered first','Which team has the most people','Alphabetical order of server names','Risk and impact in the actual environment'],3,'Prioritize the consequences and likelihood of exposure rather than treating every gap as equally urgent. Assign owners and verify the resulting fixes.'],
 ['zero-trust','A developer moves from the office to a personal hotspot. A resource checks identity, device health and requested action in both places. What principle is being applied?',['Network location alone does not confer trust','Internal addresses must always be blocked','MFA replaces authorization','Every user must have a private subnet'],0,'Zero trust evaluates access to the resource rather than granting implicit trust because a request originates inside a perimeter.'],
 ['zero-trust','A resource policy now denies a user, but an existing connection continues carrying data because the gateway ignores termination instructions. Which component’s behavior most directly needs correction?',['Certificate authority','Policy enforcement point','Identity proofing service','Risk register'],1,'The enforcement point controls the actual connection. A correct policy decision is ineffective if enforcement does not apply it.'],
 ['zero-trust','A company narrows an application identity from access to every database to access to one required table. Which principle explains the improvement?',['Permanent trust','Implicit authorization','Least privilege','Public-key recovery'],2,'The identity receives only the access needed for its task, reducing the impact of misuse or compromise. Machine identities need least privilege too.'],
 ['physical-security','Vehicles can reach a building’s glass reception area directly from the road. Which proposal addresses the vehicle-impact risk most directly?',['More detailed visitor logs','A longer badge PIN','A camera recording at higher resolution','Appropriately rated vehicle barriers with a safe entry design'],3,'Physical barriers address vehicle entry. Logging and cameras improve evidence but do not stop the impact described.'],
 ['physical-security','A secure room uses a door alarm that is repeatedly triggered by a misaligned frame. Staff begin ignoring it. What is the best next step?',['Repair the cause and validate reliable detection and response','Silence the alarm permanently without another control','Treat every alarm as proof of an attack','Replace the visitor policy only'],0,'Reliable detection requires functioning sensors and an effective response. Frequent nuisance alarms undermine both; removing detection without replacement leaves a gap.'],
 ['physical-security','A delivery driver follows an employee through a controlled entrance. Which additional design best helps enforce individual passage?',['A poster inside the building','A properly operated access-control vestibule','A weekly backup test','An encrypted visitor spreadsheet'],1,'A vestibule can control passage between interlocked doors. Procedures and supervision still matter; encryption of visitor records does not control entry.'],
 ['deception-disruption','A security team places a fake contract file where ordinary work should never access it and alerts on opening. Which tool is being used?',['Key escrow','Gap baseline','Honeyfile','Access-control list'],2,'A honeyfile is bait whose unexpected use can indicate suspicious activity. The alert should lead to investigation rather than automatic attribution.'],
 ['deception-disruption','Analysts want attackers to encounter several isolated decoy services that resemble a small enterprise. Which approach fits better than a single decoy host?',['Data masking','A directive policy','A certificate chain','A honeynet'],3,'A honeynet presents multiple connected decoy systems. Isolation and monitoring keep the deception environment from becoming a path to production.'],
 ['deception-disruption','A fake credential triggers an alert when used, but also accidentally grants access to a real billing API. What is the design flaw?',['The decoy creates genuine access instead of merely exposing suspicious use','All decoys require administrator permissions','Alerts must never be linked to credentials','Honeytokens cannot be strings'],0,'A honeytoken should not introduce useful production privileges. Deception needs containment as well as detection.']
]);
mcBankAdd('1.3',[
 ['change-management','A firewall replacement is technically simple but would interrupt a warehouse’s busiest dispatch hour. What should the change owner resolve before seeking approval?',['Only which engineer prefers the task','Business impact and a suitable agreed implementation window','Only the replacement device’s color','Whether the ticket contains a vendor logo'],1,'Technical effort and business impact are different. Involve the affected workflow owners and plan the disruption before approval.'],
 ['change-management','A team submits a request with a benefit statement but no rollback method or validation criteria. What is the most useful response from the approval authority?',['Approve because benefits are documented','Reject all future requests from the team','Request recovery and success criteria before deciding','Treat the missing sections as completed testing'],2,'Approvers need evidence about both expected value and failure handling. A documented benefit alone does not make the change ready.'],
 ['change-management','A maintenance task affects a shared login service. The request lists only the application being updated. What important scope is missing?',['The engineer’s preferred workstation','The vendor’s marketing roadmap','The next password-expiry date','Other services and users that depend on that login service'],3,'Shared dependencies can widen disruption beyond the named application. Stakeholder and impact analysis should include downstream consumers.'],
 ['change-management','Testing reveals that an upgrade irreversibly converts a database schema. The backout plan says only “reinstall the old application.” What should change?',['Include a tested data-recovery method compatible with the old version','Shorten the outage estimate without retesting','Delete the test result because installation succeeded','Keep the plan because software reinstall always reverses data changes'],0,'Application rollback may not undo data transformations. Recovery must cover the data state and its compatibility, not just application binaries.'],
 ['change-management','After an urgent authorized fix, a technician wants to omit the record because service is restored. What is the best follow-up?',['Erase temporary notes to avoid confusion','Document what changed, validate service and complete the required review','Wait for the next outage before documenting','Assume emergency changes have no owner'],1,'Urgency may change the approval path, but it does not remove the need for accountability, validation and an accurate configuration record.'],
 ['change-management','Two teams each plan approved work on systems that depend on one another during the same hour. What should the coordinator do?',['Assume separate approvals guarantee compatibility','Choose whichever ticket was created first without analysis','Resolve sequencing and combined impact with both owners','Cancel all maintenance permanently'],2,'Individually approved changes can conflict. Coordinating dependencies and recovery plans avoids avoidable combined outages.'],
 ['change-management','A test plan says the change is successful if a service starts. Users also need to submit and retrieve orders. What improvement best validates the real outcome?',['Check only the software version','Increase the ticket’s priority','Count the number of reboot messages','Test the critical end-to-end order workflow'],3,'A running process may still fail its business function. Validation should exercise the required user transactions and dependencies.'],
 ['change-management','A rollout begins producing errors at the threshold defined in the approved recovery plan. The engineer wants to keep trying undocumented fixes. What is the strongest next action?',['Follow the agreed stop and recovery decision process','Ignore the threshold until all machines are changed','Close the ticket before investigating','Treat approval as permission for any subsequent modification'],0,'Predefined triggers prevent uncontrolled troubleshooting from extending harm. If circumstances require a different response, use the authorized decision path.'],
 ['technical-change-management','An update replaces an executable allowed by its old file hash. The new version is blocked despite being legitimate. What should be investigated first?',['Whether the employee’s badge expired','Whether the approved allow-list entry was updated for the new binary','Whether the backup retention period is too long','Whether the old diagram uses the wrong font'],1,'A content hash changes with the binary. Validate the new executable and the intended enforcement rule rather than disabling protection broadly.'],
 ['technical-change-management','A service configuration is edited successfully, but the running process still uses the old values. The documentation says it reads settings only on startup. What was missed?',['A certificate revocation request','A domain-wide password reset','The required service restart and associated outage planning','A new risk-assessment framework'],2,'Some changes take effect only after a restart. Include the restart, dependent service effects and verification in the implementation plan.'],
 ['technical-change-management','A workstation update requires two reboots and a health check. A schedule accounts only for the five-minute installation. Which estimate is incomplete?',['The inventory count','The vendor’s release number','The file’s checksum','The full maintenance-window duration'],3,'The window must account for effective implementation, restarts, verification and recovery, not just time inside the installer.'],
 ['technical-change-management','A business application requires a library version incompatible with a proposed OS upgrade. What should the team do before deployment?',['Test and plan a supported combination or application remediation','Force the upgrade because the OS is newer','Remove the dependency from documentation','Assume a successful download proves compatibility'],0,'Compatibility and business dependency constrain the rollout. Unsupported combinations can break service even when installation itself works.'],
 ['technical-change-management','An outage follows three undocumented edits to a router. Which practice would most improve the ability to isolate and reverse the faulty edit?',['Changing all settings again at once','Keeping reviewed configuration revisions and a known baseline','Renaming the device after each edit','Recording only that a change occurred'],1,'Versioned configurations expose precise differences and preserve previous states. A generic note does not identify which setting caused the problem.'],
 ['technical-change-management','After a subnet redesign, the monitoring instructions still refer to removed addresses. What should be part of completing the change?',['Leaving old instructions as the only copy','Disabling monitoring until a future project','Updating diagrams and operating procedures with controlled history','Changing unrelated user permissions'],2,'Operational documentation must reflect the actual environment. History can remain available without confusing old instructions with current ones.'],
 ['technical-change-management','A new deny rule blocks an integration used by a finance application. The rule works exactly as configured. What did pre-change preparation most likely miss?',['The need to make every rule permissive','The application’s branding','The staff training attendance count','A required traffic dependency and a test of that workflow'],3,'A technically valid rule can still violate business requirements. Identify necessary flows and validate dependent applications before and after implementation.'],
 ['technical-change-management','A technician makes a successful change directly in production but never updates the managed configuration file. What is the likely future problem?',['The next automated deployment may overwrite the change with stale settings','Version control automatically discovers every live change','The production system no longer needs backups','The change is protected merely because it succeeded once'],0,'Managed desired state and actual state can diverge. Record the authorized change in the configuration source and validate subsequent deployment behavior.']
]);
mcBankAdd('1.4',[
 ['public-key-infrastructure','An archive service should be able to receive confidential submissions from anyone, while only the archive can decrypt them. Which key should it distribute for public-key encryption?',['Its private decryption key','Its public encryption key','Every submitter’s private signing key','A plaintext copy of the archive password'],1,'Senders encrypt for the archive using its authenticated public key. The archive retains the corresponding private key for decryption.'],
 ['public-key-infrastructure','A former administrator may still possess a copy of a critical private key. Reissuing a certificate with the same key is proposed. What is the flaw?',['The new certificate will have no expiration','Public keys become unusable after an employee leaves','The old private-key copy can still be used; replace the compromised key material too','A new issuer signature makes every old private-key copy vanish'],2,'A new certificate does not invalidate an exposed private key’s mathematical capabilities. Replace the key pair and manage the old certificate’s trust appropriately.'],
 ['public-key-infrastructure','A company needs disaster recovery for encrypted records but worries about misuse of recovery keys. Which design best addresses both needs?',['Unrestricted keys in the same shared folder as the records','Destroying all recovery material','A key copy known to every help-desk employee','Controlled escrow with restricted access, approval and auditing'],3,'Escrow provides recovery, while access controls and oversight reduce misuse. Availability of recovery keys and confidentiality of those keys both matter.'],
 ['encrypting-data','A database volume is encrypted, but a reporting account can query every confidential row. Which requirement is still unmet?',['Authorization limiting what that account may retrieve','Encryption of unused sectors','A longer volume label','A second copy of the volume key beside the database'],0,'Storage encryption does not limit an authorized database session’s queries. Permissions and application controls must enforce record access.'],
 ['encrypting-data','A branch sends sensitive records through a protected tunnel, then exports them to an unencrypted USB drive. Where is the newly exposed data?',['Only in transit inside the tunnel','At rest on the removable drive','Only in the branch’s public certificate','Only in the tunnel’s routing table'],1,'Transport protection ends at its endpoints. The saved export needs its own storage protection and access controls.'],
 ['encrypting-data','A stolen encrypted laptop was powered on with an unlocked user session. Why should responders not assume encryption prevented all disclosure?',['Encryption never protects laptop storage','The certificate must already be expired','The running session may have access to decrypted content and keys','Only symmetric encryption works while powered off'],2,'Protection against offline storage theft is different from protection of an active authenticated session. Assess session access and exposed data.'],
 ['key-exchange','Two parties derive a session secret from exchanged public values and private local contributions. What should remain local?',['All routing information','The names of the algorithms','The peer’s public value','Each party’s private contribution'],3,'Public exchange values can travel over the network, while private contributions remain secret. The resulting shared material is derived rather than simply sent in plaintext.'],
 ['key-exchange','A key agreement succeeds, but neither side authenticates the other. An intermediary negotiates a separate secret with each side. Which missing protection enabled this?',['Authentication binding the exchange to the intended peer','A larger attachment limit','A shorter session identifier','A public record of both private values'],0,'Agreement on secrets does not itself prove identity. Authentication is needed to detect substitution of a peer’s exchange.'],
 ['encryption-technologies','A certificate issuer wants signing operations to occur in dedicated hardware without routinely exposing private keys to general-purpose application memory. Which technology best fits?',['A plain file share','A hardware security module','A public CRL mirror','An unencrypted configuration repository'],1,'An HSM protects cryptographic key material and performs operations within its security boundary. A public status service does not replace private-key protection.'],
 ['encryption-technologies','A laptop binds disk-unlock behavior to trusted platform measurements. Which hardware component most directly supports those measurements and device-bound keys?',['A load balancer','A certificate transparency log','A TPM','A network tap'],2,'A trusted platform module supports platform measurements and protected key use. It does not guarantee that all applications are safe.'],
 ['encryption-technologies','An application processes sensitive information inside an isolated protected execution area on the device. Which concept is being used?',['Token substitution','CRL distribution','Port mirroring','A secure enclave'],3,'A secure enclave isolates sensitive computation and data from other software. Tokenization and status publication solve different problems.'],
 ['obfuscation','A customer-service screen shows only the final digits of a stored bank number while authorized payment processing retains the full value. Which technique describes the display?',['Data masking','Hash-based authentication','Key agreement','Steganography'],0,'Masking conceals selected parts from a viewer. It does not necessarily replace or encrypt the underlying stored value.'],
 ['obfuscation','A payment application exchanges a random surrogate with merchants; a protected service retains the mapping to the real account. What should be most tightly protected?',['Only the surrogate’s font','The mapping service and its access paths','Only the merchant’s display resolution','The public names of the algorithms'],1,'Tokenization moves sensitivity into the mapping and recovery system. Possession of a token must not casually grant access to its underlying value.'],
 ['obfuscation','An investigator finds a hidden message encoded inside an otherwise ordinary audio recording. The message itself is readable after extraction. What distinction does this illustrate?',['Hashing always conceals message existence','Encryption and concealment are identical','Steganography can hide existence without encrypting content','A digital signature hides all attachments'],2,'The carrier conceals the message’s presence. Confidentiality of the extracted content would require a separate protection such as encryption.'],
 ['hashing-digital-signatures','A vendor’s trusted release digest and a downloaded package’s digest differ. What is the strongest immediate conclusion?',['The mismatch identifies a particular attacker','The file is safe because its filename matches','The digest can be decrypted to repair the file','The package does not match that trusted release; do not treat it as verified'],3,'A mismatch signals different content or an incorrect comparison. It does not alone identify the cause, location or actor responsible.'],
 ['hashing-digital-signatures','Two accounts use the same password but have different random salts. Why should their stored password-hash results differ?',['The salt changes the input to the password-hashing process','The salt encrypts the username with a public key','Different salts make guessing impossible','The database must discard each hash after one login'],0,'Unique salts make identical passwords yield different stored results and reduce reuse of precomputed work. They do not eliminate guessing attacks.'],
 ['hashing-digital-signatures','A developer sees that a signed update has the expected publisher identity and unchanged signed bytes. Which further claim is not established by those facts?',['The signature supports source authentication','The code is guaranteed to contain no vulnerability','The signed content matches the verification operation','The signing key’s custody still matters'],1,'A signature is evidence about origin and integrity. Code can still be unsafe because of defects or a compromised development process.'],
 ['blockchain-technology','A ledger entry recorded a sensor’s incorrect temperature reading. Every participant agreed on that entry and no one altered it afterward. What limitation remains?',['Distributed copies cannot retain history','Hashes reveal the correct physical reading','Agreement and tamper evidence do not prove the original input was accurate','Consensus automatically fixes every faulty sensor'],2,'A verifiable record of an input does not make that input true. Trustworthy collection and validation are still required.'],
 ['blockchain-technology','A participant edits an old transaction but leaves later blocks’ references unchanged. Why is the altered history inconsistent?',['All blocks must have the same timestamp','Transactions can only contain public keys','The ledger’s encryption password changes','The edited block’s digest no longer matches the later hash reference'],3,'Hash links make changes to earlier content detectable through inconsistent references. Acceptance of a history also depends on the network’s validation rules.'],
 ['certificates','A server sends a certificate for reports.example.test when the browser requested payroll.example.test. The issuer is trusted and the dates are valid. Which check still fails?',['The requested hostname must be covered by the certificate','Every certificate must use the same hostname','The browser must possess the server’s private key','The certificate must omit its subject'],0,'A trusted issuer does not authorize a certificate for unrelated names. Validation includes the requested identity as well as chain and validity checks.'],
 ['certificates','An admin is about to email a CSR and the matching private key to a certificate issuer. Which correction is necessary?',['Remove the public key from the CSR','Send the request but retain and protect the private key','Include the private key only if the request is urgent','Send every server’s private key to establish trust'],1,'The issuer needs the public-key request information. Transmitting the private key unnecessarily expands who can impersonate the key owner.'],
 ['certificates','A client trusts a root CA, but the server omits a needed intermediate certificate and the client cannot obtain it elsewhere. What can happen?',['Hostname checks become unnecessary','The leaf turns into a private key','Chain construction or validation can fail despite a valid leaf signature','The client must automatically trust any new root'],2,'The client needs a valid path to a trusted anchor. Missing chain information can prevent it from establishing that path.'],
 ['certificates','A certificate remains within its validity dates, but the CA has revoked it after compromise. Which statement is accurate?',['Expiration is the only status that matters','Revocation changes the certificate’s hostname','Revocation means the public key becomes secret','An unexpired certificate may still be unacceptable because it was revoked'],3,'Validity dates alone do not establish current trust. Revocation communicates a reason to stop relying on a certificate before normal expiry.'],
 ['certificates','A server supplies a signed, fresh OCSP response alongside its certificate. Why does the client still validate that response?',['The server’s delivery does not replace verification of the responder’s signature and status freshness','The response contains the server’s private key','Stapling automatically removes the need for any certificate checks','The client must rewrite the status before using it'],0,'Stapling changes how status reaches the client. Authenticity, applicability and freshness of the status still need validation.']
]);
mcBankAdd('2.1',[
 ['threat-actors','A campaign steals diplomatic research without demanding payment and repeatedly targets organizations of strategic interest to a government. Which actor best fits the stated objective?',['A random accidental insider','A financially focused carding group','A nation-state or state-sponsored actor','A department using unapproved storage'],2,'Strategic espionage is consistent with state interests. Evidence about sponsorship still matters; the goal supports a classification, not attribution to a specific country.'],
 ['threat-actors','A group offers stolen customer records for sale and reinvests proceeds in more attacks. Which motivation is most clearly demonstrated?',['Ideology','Public safety','Revenge against one employee','Financial gain'],3,'The stated behavior is profit-seeking. Sophistication alone would not establish motive, but selling the data provides direct evidence here.'],
 ['threat-actors','A dismissed employee uses still-active credentials to destroy project files. Which combination best describes the actor and motivation?',['Insider threat and revenge','Shadow IT and convenience','Nation-state and espionage','Hacktivist and research'],0,'The former employee abuses a trusted access relationship for retaliation. Promptly removing unnecessary access limits this opportunity.'],
 ['threat-actors','A marketing team adopts an unapproved analytics service and uploads company data to meet a deadline. What is the most direct classification?',['An external espionage campaign','Shadow IT','Organized extortion','A destructive state operation'],1,'Shadow IT is technology use outside authorized governance. It can be risky without malicious intent.'],
 ['threat-actors','A beginner runs a purchased toolkit against exposed systems without understanding how the exploits work. What capability assessment is reasonable?',['Low skill guarantees low impact','The toolkit proves government sponsorship','Packaged tools can give a low-skill actor effective attack capability','The activity must be legitimate because the tools were purchased'],2,'Attack tooling can lower the expertise required to cause harm. Skill and available capability are related but not identical.'],
 ['threat-actors','An activist group disrupts a company’s public site and announces a political demand. Which actor category is most supported?',['Accidental insider','Shadow IT','Ordinary authorized contractor','Hacktivist'],3,'An ideological or political objective is characteristic of hacktivism. Disruption alone would not establish that motivation without the additional context.'],
 ['threat-actors','An employee unknowingly shares an internal spreadsheet through a public link. What does this show about insider risk?',['It can result from mistakes as well as malicious conduct','It always requires advanced malware','It is impossible when authentication works','It only exists after someone leaves the company'],0,'Insiders can create exposure unintentionally through legitimate access. Intent is important for assessment and response but is not required for harm.'],
 ['threat-actors','A vendor technician has approved administrative access to a customer’s systems and misuses it. Which factor most directly increases the risk?',['The vendor’s website uses a modern design','The trusted privileged access available to that individual','The technician works outside office hours','The customer’s logo is publicly visible'],1,'The existing access relationship provides a powerful route to harm. An actor need not be a direct employee to misuse entrusted access.'],
 ['threat-actors','A ransomware operation negotiates payments, uses affiliates and shares revenue. Which classification best matches that business model?',['An unintentional configuration error','A personal productivity shortcut','Organized financially motivated crime','An internal compliance assessment'],2,'The coordinated revenue model supports organized crime. The tools or location of infrastructure alone would not identify its members.'],
 ['threat-actors','A sophisticated intrusion uses a tool that is freely available worldwide. Which inference is weakest?',['The actor had access to that tool','The organization should investigate the intrusion','The tool can support multiple actor types','The tool alone proves the actor belongs to one named country'],3,'Widely available tooling is not unique attribution evidence. Combine technical, behavioral and contextual evidence rather than assigning a sponsor from one tool.'],
 ['threat-actors','An external attacker and a payroll employee could both steal salary data. What advantage might the employee already have?',['Legitimate access and knowledge of internal processes','Guaranteed immunity from monitoring','Ownership of every encryption key','Automatic control over all external services'],0,'Existing permissions and knowledge can reduce barriers for an insider. They do not imply unlimited access or that detection is impossible.'],
 ['threat-actors','A group quietly collects industrial designs for years. Another group immediately advertises stolen payment data for sale. What distinction is best supported by the behavior?',['Only the second group can be external','Their apparent motivations differ: espionage versus profit','The first group cannot use malware','The second group must be less technically skilled'],1,'Behavior supports different likely goals. It does not, by itself, prove membership, exact skill or the technical tools used.'],
 ['threat-actors','A team evaluates an adversary using funding, staffing, technical knowledge and available infrastructure. What is the assessment mainly characterizing?',['The victim’s password policy','Whether the actor’s conduct was authorized','The actor’s resources and capability','The attacker’s exact physical location'],2,'Resources and skill influence feasible attacks and persistence. These measures do not uniquely establish identity or location.'],
 ['threat-actors','A staff member creates a personal cloud account for work files because the approved tool seems slow. What response addresses the immediate governance problem?',['Treat the account as proof of state espionage','Allow it permanently without examining the data','Delete unrelated business applications','Assess and move the work to an approved service with appropriate controls'],3,'The behavior is unapproved technology use. Address data exposure and business needs through an approved process rather than assuming malicious motive.'],
 ['threat-actors','A contractor is angry about an unpaid invoice and threatens to leak files already obtained during authorized work. Which motive is most directly evidenced?',['Retaliation or revenge','A purely accidental mistake','National defense research','Routine asset inventory'],0,'The grievance and threat support retaliation as a motive. Legitimate original access does not authorize later disclosure.'],
 ['threat-actors','Investigators know an attack caused heavy damage but have no reliable evidence about who did it. What should their report separate?',['Internal and external users solely by IP address','Observed impact from uncertain actor attribution','Malware names from all technical evidence','The incident from any containment activity'],1,'Damage is evidence about outcome, not necessarily identity. State what is known, assess plausible actor characteristics and preserve uncertainty about attribution.']
]);
mcBankAdd('2.2',[
 ['common-threat-vectors','A collaboration platform allows external guests to send documents directly to staff. Security reviewed email attachments but not this platform. What exposure was missed?',['Only removable media','Only an unused server port','A message-based file delivery path outside email','A physical building entrance'],2,'Messaging and collaboration systems can deliver malicious links and files too. Review content handling and external access across communication channels.'],
 ['common-threat-vectors','An office finds a free promotional USB device at an event. It presents itself as a keyboard when connected. Why is ordinary file scanning alone insufficient?',['Every USB device is only passive storage','Scanning always checks future keystrokes','USB devices cannot emulate other hardware','The device may act as an input device rather than merely carrying files'],3,'Removable devices can expose interfaces beyond storage. Approved device-handling controls are needed rather than trusting a file scan alone.'],
 ['common-threat-vectors','A newly deployed appliance is internet reachable and still accepts the manufacturer’s published administrator login. Which fix most directly removes this easy entry path?',['Replace or disable the default credentials and restrict administrative exposure','Change the browser’s theme','Add a longer device description','Wait for evidence of data theft'],0,'Known default access and broad exposure create avoidable entry opportunities. Address the credentials and who can reach management.'],
 ['common-threat-vectors','A business keeps an unsupported file-transfer service because one customer still uses it. Which issue should drive the risk discussion?',['Old software cannot process data','Reachable unsupported software may retain exploitable flaws without vendor fixes','Only supported software has an attack surface','The customer’s location determines whether patches matter'],1,'The business dependency does not remove exposure. Restrict access and plan supported replacement or other validated mitigation.'],
 ['common-threat-vectors','A media-processing server opens uploaded pictures automatically. What should security assess besides the filename extension?',['Only the uploader’s preferred language','Only the image dimensions','The parser’s vulnerabilities and handling of untrusted content','Only whether the picture looks normal to a person'],2,'File processors can contain exploitable defects. A harmless-looking image or extension does not establish that its contents are safe to parse.'],
 ['common-threat-vectors','A review finds an unused management daemon listening on a public interface. The service is fully patched. Why remove it if unnecessary?',['Patches make authentication impossible','Every open port proves an existing compromise','Closing it eliminates all future risk on the server','It removes an unneeded reachable entry point'],3,'Reducing exposed functionality shrinks attack surface even for currently patched services. It does not eliminate unrelated risks.'],
 ['phishing','A text message claims a delivery is delayed and directs an employee to enter credentials on a lookalike site. Which channel-specific term applies?',['Smishing','Vishing','Watering hole','Physical tailgating'],0,'Smishing is phishing delivered through SMS or text messaging. The deceptive goal can be the same as an email-based attack.'],
 ['phishing','A caller claiming to be bank fraud support asks the victim to read out a sign-in code. What is the safest response?',['Provide it if caller ID shows the bank','End the call and contact the bank through an independently known route','Provide half the code as a test','Reply through a link texted by the caller'],1,'Voice phishing can imitate trusted numbers and urgent fraud alerts. A separate trusted contact route avoids relying on attacker-provided verification.'],
 ['phishing','An email to a specific engineer references a real project and asks them to open a “revised specification.” What makes this spear phishing if malicious?',['It includes a file of any kind','It arrives during business hours','It is tailored to a particular target or group','It must contain a misspelled company name'],2,'Specific targeting and relevant context distinguish spear phishing. Accurate information and polished writing do not make the message authentic.'],
 ['phishing','A finance employee receives a payment change request from a real executive mailbox that was taken over. Why is checking the sender address alone inadequate?',['Real mailboxes cannot send links','Only spoofed addresses can be malicious','The display name is always stronger evidence','A compromised legitimate account can be used for business email compromise'],3,'A valid account can carry a fraudulent request. Sensitive changes need independent verification using established procedures.'],
 ['phishing','A request targets a CEO with a plausible confidential acquisition document. What term describes phishing aimed at high-profile senior individuals?',['Whaling','Rooting','Tokenization','DLL injection'],0,'Whaling is highly targeted phishing aimed at senior or prominent people. The document’s plausibility may increase persuasion without proving trust.'],
 ['impersonation','Someone claiming to be the CIO asks the help desk to reset MFA and says the normal checks are beneath their authority. What should the technician do?',['Accept the title as identity proof','Use the established independent identity-verification process','Send the reset link to any address the caller provides','Ask only for the CIO’s publicly listed name'],1,'Claimed authority is a persuasion technique, not verification. Bypassing checks is exactly the outcome an impersonator may seek.'],
 ['impersonation','An attacker invents a story about an audit to justify collecting employee identity documents. What is the fabricated context called?',['A certificate chain','A memory boundary','A pretext','A hash collision'],2,'A pretext creates a plausible reason for the victim to cooperate. Verify the request and its authorization through trusted channels.'],
 ['impersonation','A supplier payment request says “Do not call anyone; this must be completed in five minutes.” What combination of influence is being used?',['Availability and integrity','Encryption and signing','Patching and isolation','Urgency and discouraging independent verification'],3,'Time pressure and isolation reduce deliberation. Established approval and verification steps should still apply.'],
 ['watering-hole-attacks','An attacker cannot reach a research network directly, so it compromises a professional forum its researchers routinely visit. Why choose that forum?',['It brings the intended victims to the attacker-controlled content during normal activity','It proves every forum visitor works for the target','It grants automatic administrator rights on every device','It replaces the need for any exploit or deception'],0,'A watering hole exploits the target group’s habits. Visiting the compromised location creates exposure; further conditions determine actual compromise.'],
 ['watering-hole-attacks','A familiar trade website begins offering an unexpected security-plugin download. What should a visitor do?',['Install it because the domain is familiar','Use an approved independent update source and report the prompt','Disable browser protections before downloading','Forward the installer to colleagues for convenience'],1,'A reputable site can be compromised. Familiarity does not authenticate unexpected executable content.'],
 ['other-social-engineering-attacks','A fabricated service outage notice is deliberately spread to cause panic and reputational harm. Which term most precisely captures the knowingly false campaign?',['Ordinary misinformation without intent','Accounting','Disinformation','Certificate validation'],2,'Disinformation involves deliberate deception. Someone unknowingly repeating a false claim may instead be spreading misinformation.'],
 ['other-social-engineering-attacks','An employee forwards a false security rumor believing it is true. How does this differ from the actor who knowingly invented it?',['The employee’s message is encrypted by default','Only the employee’s message can cause harm','Belief makes the claim accurate','The employee spreads misinformation without necessarily intending deception'],3,'Intent separates knowingly deceptive disinformation from inaccurate information shared in good faith. Both can mislead recipients.'],
 ['other-social-engineering-attacks','A social-media account copies a vendor’s branding and offers support if users supply recovery codes. Which action is appropriate?',['Reach the vendor through its verified support channel and do not provide the codes','Trust the matching logo','Send one recovery code to test the account','Assume follower count authenticates support staff'],0,'Brand impersonation borrows trust signals without proving identity. Recovery codes grant access and should not be disclosed to an unverified contact.'],
 ['common-threat-vectors','A remote worker’s personal tablet can reach the same internal services as managed company devices, despite unknown security settings. What is the best control focus?',['Trust every device owned by a current employee','Assess device posture and limit access according to the approved policy','Disable accounting only on personal devices','Assume a successful password proves device safety'],1,'User identity and device trust are different inputs. Unmanaged endpoints need access decisions that account for their posture and the resources requested.']
]);
mcBankAdd('2.3',[
 ['memory-injections','An endpoint alert shows one process writing executable content into another process’s memory and starting execution there. Which activity best explains the alert?',['Routine certificate renewal','Data masking','Memory injection','A DNS lookup'],2,'The suspicious behavior crosses process memory and execution boundaries. The destination process’s trusted name does not establish that all executing code is trustworthy.'],
 ['memory-injections','A signed desktop program unexpectedly loads an unauthorized DLL and begins making unusual network connections. What should the investigation avoid assuming?',['The loaded module may matter','Execution behavior provides useful evidence','The process can host code from other components','The signed program name guarantees everything running inside it is benign'],3,'A trusted executable can load or be manipulated into running malicious code. Investigate modules and behavior, not only the displayed process name.'],
 ['buffer-overflows','A service copies a network field into a fixed-size buffer without checking its length, overwriting adjacent memory. Which flaw is present?',['Buffer overflow','Stored XSS','Certificate revocation','A legitimate key exchange'],0,'Writing beyond a buffer’s allocated boundary can corrupt memory and sometimes alter execution. Input-length checks and safer memory handling address this defect.'],
 ['buffer-overflows','A developer fixes a parser by rejecting input longer than its destination buffer and using bounds-checked operations. Which risk is most directly reduced?',['A revoked certificate being trusted','Out-of-bounds writes from oversized input','A customer choosing a weak password','An unauthorized mobile app installation'],1,'Bounds enforcement prevents writes beyond the intended memory region. Other security controls remain necessary for unrelated attack paths.'],
 ['race-conditions','An application checks permission on a file, then opens it later. Between those steps, another process replaces the file with a sensitive target. What kind of weakness was exploited?',['An unsigned checksum','A default password','A time-of-check to time-of-use race','A hostname mismatch'],2,'The security-relevant state changed between checking and using it. The operation needs a design that binds the check to the object actually used.'],
 ['race-conditions','Two simultaneous purchase requests both see one item in stock and both decrement the count. What kind of change best addresses the underlying flaw?',['A longer web-page title','A new TLS certificate','A more detailed error message alone','Atomic transaction or synchronization of the check-and-update sequence'],3,'Concurrent operations must not act on stale shared state independently. Making the relevant sequence atomic or properly synchronized addresses the timing issue.'],
 ['malicious-updates','An attacker compromises a software publisher’s build process so customers receive harmful code through the normal update channel. What makes this especially dangerous?',['The trusted update workflow becomes a delivery path','Updates cannot be monitored','All signed software is necessarily safe','The attack requires every customer to disable networking'],0,'Users and systems may trust the normal update route. Protecting the build and distribution chain matters as well as validating received packages.'],
 ['malicious-updates','A vendor confirms its latest update may contain injected code. Which response best balances security and maintenance?',['Install it faster because newer is always safer','Follow verified vendor guidance, contain exposure and validate a clean recovery or update path','Disable all future patching permanently','Ignore the notice if the installer finished successfully'],1,'Respond to the specific compromised release through trusted guidance and investigation. Permanently abandoning updates would create other vulnerabilities.'],
 ['operating-system-vulnerabilities','A server runs a supported OS but has missed security updates for a known remotely exploitable flaw. Which conclusion is correct?',['Vendor support means the server is already patched','The flaw disappears if the server is renamed','Supported software can remain vulnerable when fixes are not deployed','Only end-of-life systems can be exploited'],2,'Support makes fixes available; it does not install or validate them. Use the approved patch and mitigation process for the affected exposure.'],
 ['operating-system-vulnerabilities','A business cannot patch a critical OS flaw until a dependency is tested. Which interim action best reduces risk?',['Assume testing removes the exposure','Publish the service more broadly for observation','Turn off audit logging to improve performance','Restrict the vulnerable feature or access path using validated mitigations'],3,'Compensating measures reduce exposure while a tested fix is prepared. They should be monitored and followed by permanent remediation.'],
 ['sql-injection','A search handler builds a database query by joining text with user input. Crafted input changes the query’s meaning. Which correction most directly addresses this?',['Use parameterized queries that keep data separate from query structure','Encrypt only the network connection','Hide the search box with CSS','Grant the application broader database permissions'],0,'Bound parameters keep supplied values from becoming SQL structure. Transport encryption does not correct unsafe query construction.'],
 ['sql-injection','An application account can read only its own reporting tables. An injection flaw is later discovered. Why was the restricted database role still valuable?',['It proves injection cannot occur','It limits what the compromised query context can access or change','It makes input validation unnecessary','It automatically patches the vulnerable handler'],1,'Least privilege can reduce impact, even though it does not eliminate the injection defect. Fix the query handling too.'],
 ['cross-site-scripting','A support ticket stores untrusted markup that executes when an agent opens the ticket later. Which combination best describes this?',['SQL injection on the database server','A buffer overflow in the kernel','Stored XSS executing in a viewer’s browser','A certificate revocation event'],2,'The payload persists in application content and later executes in the browser context when rendered unsafely. It is distinct from changing a database query.'],
 ['cross-site-scripting','A page must display customer input as ordinary text. Which implementation choice most directly prevents it from being interpreted as active markup?',['Trusting all signed-in customers','Using HTTPS without changing rendering','Removing the input’s filename extension','Using safe text rendering or context-appropriate output encoding'],3,'Data intended as text should remain text in the destination context. Encryption of transport does not change how the browser interprets page content.'],
 ['hardware-vulnerabilities','A router’s operating system is current, but its low-level firmware has a vendor-confirmed flaw. What should the asset maintenance process recognize?',['Security updates can be required below the operating-system layer','OS patching automatically replaces every firmware component','Hardware cannot contain software defects','Changing the router’s hostname fixes firmware'],0,'Firmware controls hardware behavior and can contain exploitable defects. Track supported firmware versions and validated updates as part of maintenance.'],
 ['hardware-vulnerabilities','A device still performs its job, but the manufacturer has ended security support. What makes replacement planning important?',['Working equipment always has no risk','Future vulnerabilities may have no vendor fix despite continued functionality','Its public IP address must have changed','The device can no longer produce logs'],1,'Functional operation and support status are different. Consider exposure, compensating controls and a migration plan rather than equating “works” with “maintained.”'],
 ['virtualization-vulnerabilities','A guest VM exploit reaches the hypervisor and affects other workloads. What boundary was breached?',['The distinction between two file extensions','A certificate’s validity dates','Isolation between the guest and its host environment','The requirement for a public DNS name'],2,'A VM escape crosses the intended guest boundary. Hypervisor security is important because multiple workloads rely on that isolation.'],
 ['virtualization-vulnerabilities','A cloud tenant receives reassigned storage containing readable remnants from a previous tenant. Which process failed most directly?',['Public-key distribution','Password expiration','Guest hostname selection','Resource sanitization and isolation before reuse'],3,'Reused resources must not expose another workload’s residual data. Reassignment alone does not guarantee old contents are inaccessible.'],
 ['cloud-specific-vulnerabilities','A web application moves to a managed cloud platform but still constructs unsafe database queries. What changes about its injection risk?',['The application flaw remains until its query handling is corrected','Cloud hosting automatically rewrites every query','Only on-premises software can be injected','The flaw becomes a certificate problem'],0,'A hosting change does not remove insecure application logic. Customer-controlled code still requires secure design and testing.'],
 ['cloud-specific-vulnerabilities','A workload identity can administer storage belonging to unrelated projects because of an overly broad customer policy. Who must address that policy?',['Only internet visitors','The team responsible for the customer-controlled permissions','Only the hardware manufacturer','No one if the cloud provider is reputable'],1,'Shared responsibility does not remove the customer’s responsibility for permissions it controls. Narrow the role and investigate possible misuse.'],
 ['supply-chain-vulnerabilities','A trusted service provider is compromised and its remote support tooling is used to reach several customers. Which relationship carried the attack?',['Only the customers’ public DNS records','A normal certificate renewal','Privileged access inherited through the supplier relationship','An unrelated physical sensor'],2,'A provider’s legitimate management path can extend a compromise downstream. Constrain, monitor and review supplier access.'],
 ['supply-chain-vulnerabilities','A development team adds a third-party package without reviewing its provenance. A malicious version is later included in production. Which control area was most directly overlooked?',['Employee parking access','Display masking of phone numbers','Printer paper handling','Software dependency and supplier assurance'],3,'Dependencies introduce upstream trust into the product. Track sources and versions, review changes and respond to component risk.'],
 ['misconfiguration-vulnerabilities','A supported, fully patched database is publicly readable because of a deployment template. What is the root issue described?',['An insecure access configuration, not necessarily a new software flaw','Proof that all encryption is broken','A guaranteed nation-state intrusion','A buffer overflow caused by the hostname'],0,'Correct software can still expose data through permissive settings. Correct the template and validate deployed instances to stop repeated exposure.'],
 ['misconfiguration-vulnerabilities','A secure server baseline was approved last month. A local change now enables an unnecessary service, and no one notices. What would most directly reveal this divergence?',['Checking only the purchase date','Comparing live configuration with the approved baseline','Renewing an unrelated certificate','Changing the desktop wallpaper'],1,'Configuration drift monitoring compares actual state with intended state. Initial hardening does not guarantee settings remain unchanged.'],
 ['mobile-device-vulnerabilities','A user installs an Android application through an unapproved download while leaving the OS privilege model intact. Which term describes the installation route?',['Rooting necessarily occurred','Jailbreaking necessarily occurred','Sideloading','Certificate stapling'],2,'Sideloading is installation outside the normal approved distribution route. It is distinct from obtaining root privileges or removing platform restrictions.'],
 ['mobile-device-vulnerabilities','An iPhone has been modified to bypass its normal platform restrictions, then fails a company compliance check. Which modification is most consistent with that finding?',['A normal approved app update','A new lock-screen image','A valid certificate renewal','Jailbreaking'],3,'Jailbreaking bypasses iOS restrictions and can undermine the expected security boundary. Device compliance can inform whether access is permitted.'],
 ['zero-day-vulnerabilities','A flaw unknown to the vendor is already being exploited, and no fix is available. Which description best fits the timing?',['Zero-day exploitation','A completed patch rollout','A planned access review','Routine certificate expiry'],0,'The exploitation precedes the normal opportunity for defenders to receive and deploy a vendor fix. Exposure reduction and investigation cannot wait for patch availability.'],
 ['zero-day-vulnerabilities','A newly disclosed exploited flaw has no patch yet, but the vendor provides a tested configuration mitigation. What is the strongest immediate response?',['Wait without changing exposure until patch day','Apply applicable validated mitigation, monitor for exploitation and track the permanent fix','Treat the workaround as proof no investigation is needed','Disable all monitoring to conserve resources'],1,'A validated mitigation can reduce risk now while the team investigates and prepares permanent remediation. A workaround does not establish that compromise never occurred.']
]);
